{"id":5296,"date":"2019-01-03T16:46:36","date_gmt":"2019-01-03T21:46:36","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/?p=5296"},"modified":"2019-01-01T16:49:05","modified_gmt":"2019-01-01T21:49:05","slug":"the-magnificent-saguaro-cactus","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/2019\/01\/03\/the-magnificent-saguaro-cactus\/","title":{"rendered":"The Magnificent Saguaro Cactus"},"content":{"rendered":"\n<p>The Saguaro Cactus (pronounced Sawarro) is the largest of the cactus family and can live to be 150-200 years old.  These are found in The Sonoran Desert of Arizona and Mexico and occasionally in southern California.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p>These cactus have one tap root that only goes down about 2 feet or so and other roots that spread out just below the surface and spread out as far as the plant is tall. Although a 10 year old plant might only be about 1&#8243;-2&#8243; tall, they can grow to be 40-60 feet tall and sprout their first &#8220;arm&#8221; at about 150 years old.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p>The Saguaro get most of their moisture during the summer rainy season and can end up weighing between 3000-5000 pounds.  Arizona has strict regulations about harvesting or collecting Saguaro.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p>Once a Saguaro dies, the woody ribs can be used to build furniture, roofs, or   fences.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"405\" height=\"540\" src=\"https:\/\/i1.wp.com\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/01\/IMG_0160.jpg?fit=700%2C525&amp;ssl=1\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-image-5297\"\/><figcaption>A healthy Saguaro about 40&#8242; tall in the National Forest<\/figcaption><\/figure>\n\n\n\n<p>We hopped in the car and took a day trip down from our winter home at Rover&#8217;s Roost RV Park to visit the Arizona-Sonoran Desert Museum, the Saguaro National Forest and maybe the International Wildlife Museum.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"760\" height=\"525\" src=\"https:\/\/i2.wp.com\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/01\/Saguaro-Nat-Forest-Map.jpg?fit=700%2C483&amp;ssl=1\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-image-5306\" srcset=\"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/01\/Saguaro-Nat-Forest-Map.jpg 760w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/01\/Saguaro-Nat-Forest-Map-300x207.jpg 300w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/01\/Saguaro-Nat-Forest-Map-768x530.jpg 768w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/01\/Saguaro-Nat-Forest-Map-1024x707.jpg 1024w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 760px) 100vw, 760px\" \/><figcaption>The blue dot just to the left of Casa Grande shows our home and the red balloon about an hour southeast is the Saguaro National Forest where we spent most of the day<\/figcaption><\/figure>\n\n\n\n<p>We headed down I-10 and entered the Saguaro National Forest from the north.  Although the visitor center was closed due to the federal government shutdown, the park\/forest was open and we could wander all we wanted.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p>As usual, you can click on any of the thumbnails below to see an enlarged view and then you can scroll right or left to see the next picture. <\/p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-gallery columns-3 is-cropped wp-block-gallery-1 is-layout-flex wp-block-gallery-is-layout-flex\"><li class=\"blocks-gallery-item\"><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"720\" height=\"540\" src=\"https:\/\/i1.wp.com\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/01\/IMG_0154.jpg?fit=700%2C525&amp;ssl=1\" alt=\"\" data-id=\"5298\" data-link=\"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/?attachment_id=5298\" class=\"wp-image-5298\" srcset=\"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/01\/IMG_0154.jpg 720w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/01\/IMG_0154-300x225.jpg 300w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/01\/IMG_0154-768x576.jpg 768w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/01\/IMG_0154-1